I've got beef with the meat packing industry. Give me 90 seconds to explain. Corporate consolidation is rampant in the meat packing industry. For example, just four companies control 85% of the U.S. beef market. These corporate giants own nearly every part of the food production process, forcing farmers and ranchers to buy and sell to these companies at whatever price they determine. As a result, the majority of profits from every meat purchase goes into the pockets of corporations, not the actual farmers and ranchers who do the difficult work. This monopolization is herding farmers, ranchers, and, yes, you American consumers. As prices at the grocery store skyrocket. Our government needs to do more to stop these corporate interests from raising costs and putting thousands of family farms out of business in the process. I am leading a bill, alongside Congressman Chris Deluzio and Pat Ryan, to break up the meatpacking industry. It's called the Family Grocery and Farmer Relief Act. It would break up dominant meatpacking monopolies, rein in foreign controlled corporate giants, and end unfair pricing practices that raise costs for American families, and box out local farmers and ranchers. It finally levels the playing field and gives independent ranchers and farmers a fair shot, while at the same time, lowering grocery bills for American families. Let's build the support we need to pass this important legislation. Tell your representative to co sponsor HR 9744, by passing this bill, we can finally squash our beef with the giant companies that control the meat packing industry, and break up these monopolies.
